WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.590 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it is september 13th  1833  in the northeastern indian port city of  kolkata  it is 90 degrees outside and the air is  humid  you're in a crowd anxiously waiting  unsure if you're about to see a  historical feat of human ingenuity  or an elaborate prank  then slowly and steadily off the horizon  a brick named tuscany approaches  in it  a hundred tons of eyes  cut in blocks and stack neatly against  one another having just finished a  four-month voyage  all the way from boston  what you just witnessed was a journey  along one of the first international  temperature controlled supply chain  also known as  the cold chain  the man behind this cold chain was  frederick tudor deservedly nicknamed the  ice king  tudor started this icy empire by  harvesting natural ice in the dead of  new england winter  then delivering it first across the  united states and eventually to great  britain and india  while this journey was long and its  historical significance monumental  tudor's cold chain from a logistical  perspective was extraordinarily simple  for a long time the only commodity he  transported was ice and ice kept itself  cool as long as there was sufficient  insulation  so tudor invested heavily in these ice  houses along the ports and his business  took off  now in the two centuries that followed  the cold chain had come a long way  today the cold chain is more complex and  more global than ever before  just to give you a sense of its scale  and complexity  nowadays for a piece of imported fruit  to get to you and me it will roughly go  through 20 steps  here's a chart just to show you what  that's like  in the last three years working as a  food investor  i have seen this process in play day in  and day out in lettuce fields in soup  plants and in refrigerated trucks  delivering imported cheese to pizzerias  all across new york city  i marveled at this well-oiled machine  that connects growers manufacturers  distributors retailers and consumers  the cold chain enables us to introduce  diversity and abundance into our diet  it removes seasonality and distances  from food  it gives us choices so that food is not  just about survival but part of our  identity  that is  until this all stopped working  many of these images may look familiar  to you  bagel shops without cream cheese  starbucks without frappuccino kfc  without chicken tenders  for me a broken cold chain meant  spending hours on the phone with our  logistics providers telling them how  unacceptable it was  for them to leave frozen ready meals in  a hot truck for 24 hours too  long our coaching is susceptible to  disruptions and we've had an awful lot  of disruptions in the last two years  not only that the coaching is also  fossil fueled and incredibly energy  intensive according to the un  the food cold chain accounts for about  1.2 gigatons of co2 emissions that is  about 1 of total global annual emissions  so in between worrying about the revenue  loss from customers receiving spoiled  food and the cost associated with that  extra pack of dry eyes i now need to  budget for in the delivery  i couldn't help but wonder  how can we make this better  one solution came to mind  and it's almost too obvious and too  logical  why not simplify the supply chain do  what the industry calls near shoring  take out a few nodes here and there  shorten the distance traveled  source locally eat farm to fork it's  simple sustainable and more resilient  right  not so fast  to illustrate just how complicated the  cold chain is and how hard it is to  disentangle even a tiny portion of it  let me take you on a journey of one  commodity  walk into any western european  supermarket you will see these beautiful  uniform fresh french green beans these  beans were in the fields just 48 hours  ago in the rural villages outside bobo  du lasso in burkina faso  among many other former european  colonies in africa  these beans were hand-picked by local  manual labor when it's hot and sunny  outside and then immediately chill to  about 40 degrees fahrenheit to ensure  high export quality  they're then loaded onto refrigerated  trucks to be packed at a temperature  controlled facility  then  lastly loaded onto a jet flying directly  to paris  now what is missing from this picture of  seamless modern miracle is the fact that  many of these villages don't have paved  roads or electricity  these farmers rely  on charcoal to chill the beans instead  of refrigerators before sending the  beans to an air-conditioned facility to  be processed  many of these farmers also have come to  rely on this high value export crop as  their sole source of income  and they import shelf-stable food  for their own consumption  now if we decide that we want to  insource the beans these farmers would  lose their livelihood and the local  economy would collapse but perhaps most  ironically those who grow the beans  cannot even afford to eat them the lack  of cold storage meant that most of these  beans would rot  before the locals could get a chance to  consume them  you see  eating locally requires refrigeration  and refrigeration  is simply  too expensive  in fact scientists estimate that  as much as 40 percent of the fresh food  produced in africa is lost due to  inadequate cold chains annually  globally  the carbon footprint of food loss and  food waste is four times the carbon  footprint of the cold chain itself  so  counterintuitively for us to solve for a  less carbon intensive food ecosystem  means adding coaching capacity and  adding it quickly  and since i'm talking to about 200 of  you who will be future business leaders  i want to talk about what you can do  as your companies rethink about your  supply chain in the post-pandemic world  it is imperative that you go beyond  the cookie cutter diversification or  near shoring strategies that your  consultants keep pitching you about and  think about the whole ecosystem  resiliency comes from strengthening the  entire supply chain and it begins with  investing in the most fragile lengths  like bringing roads bringing electricity  and bringing refrigeration to those  villages in burkina faso  tudor understood  that  until every port had an ice house there  was no cross-atlantic ice trade  similarly there is no resilient global  supply chain if farmers don't have the  infrastructure to store what they've  produced  this is also good for business  better cold storage at the point of  harvest meant fresher higher quality  produce for your company  it decreases food loss and also makes  for more predictable supplies this will  save you money and is also more  sustainable  and finally there's massive social  benefits  for the local community they'll  obviously get access to better safer and  more nutritious food but more  importantly the same set of cold storage  infrastructure will also provide access  to life-saving drugs and vaccines like  what we've seen in the last two years  the pandemic gave us an opportunity  to rethink and re-imagine what the  future called change should look like  let's take a page  from the ice kings playbook and start  investing in the weakest link in this  global system  only then will we be able to provide  freshness to all  resiliently sustainably and equitably  [Applause]  [Music]

